  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about how well any modality or configuration performs. The most critical unknown is the performance floor: how well does the simplest single-modality model (RNA alone, single profile) do? Without this baseline, we cannot determine whether adding image data, multiple profiles, or more complex fusion strategies actually improves performance. 2) What this tests: config_002 (rna_only, singleprofile, scvi_n200) establishes the simplest reasonable RNA-only baseline using the most common RNA encoder in the search space. It isolates the contribution of the RNA modality with minimal confounding complexity (no image, no aggregation, no fusion). 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the single most informative measurement is the reference point against which all other design choices (adding image, switching to multiprofile, changing encoder, adding fusion) will be judged. A simple, interpretable baseline also guards against the assumption that multimodal or multiprofile models are inherently better. Every subsequent multimodal or multiprofile result can be directly compared to this number to quantify the marginal value of each added complexity dimension."
